I"<h2 id="the-question">The question</h2>

<p>As AI tools spread through radiology, it matters that someone keeps checking they still work well. Few people have asked how that checking is done today.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-did">What we did</h2>

<p>We interviewed 16 radiologists across Europe and the United States about how they monitor the AI tools they use and what gets in the way, and analysed what they told us.</p>

<h2 id="what-we-found">What we found</h2>

<p>Many AI systems are still at an early validation stage. Monitoring is usually manual, with radiologists comparing the AI output with radiology reports after the fact. That works but takes a lot of effort. The main barriers are a lack of standard guidance, limited technical tools and limited resources.</p>

<h2 id="why-it-matters">Why it matters</h2>

<p>We recommend combining several monitoring methods, setting up dedicated governance teams, and planning resources for the long term. Clearer frameworks and investment are needed so that AI actually improves clinical workflows.</p>
